  [1mClassification: bounded[0m — one existing function, one existing call site, no new structure. So I'll present a short
  design in chat rather than write a spec. One caveat: if "track who logged in" turns out to mean a real tracking
  subsystem (analytics events, a persisted log), that's new structure and I'll stop and re-classify as architectural.

  Two things are genuinely ambiguous, so I'll take them one at a time.

  [1mFirst: what does "track" mean here?[0m This decides whether we stay bounded. The fork is whether the userId just travels
  through the existing code, or whether something new receives it. Threading it through is a five-minute change you can
  redo freely; a tracking module is a new dependency and a new interface that later code will build on, so it's
  expensive to unwind.

  - [3mConsole log + return value[0m — userId appears in the existing [38;5;153mconsole.log[39m and in the returned object. Nothing new;
    still bounded.
  - [3mSend to the API[0m — userId goes into the (currently stubbed) POST body to [38;5;153mAPI_ENDPOINT[39m. Small, but it defines part of
    a request contract with a server.
  - [3mA tracking module[0m — a new [38;5;153mtrack()[39m/analytics layer that login calls. This is a new subsystem; I'd re-classify as
    architectural and we'd do the full spec flow.

  My recommendation is the first. The function is a stub that doesn't call anything yet, so the honest minimal change is
  to make userId visible where the existing output already goes — and it doesn't commit you to a request contract or a
  module boundary before there's real code behind either.
